5. Troubleshooting and Decision-Making
The main issue I ran into was expecting the first Base64 decode to give me the final answer. When the result still looked encoded, I first thought the decoder may not have worked correctly. I looked at the output more closely and noticed that it still had the structure of Base64, so I decoded it again.
After the second Base64 layer, the output looked like shifted alphabetic text instead of another Base64 string. That change helped me decide to stop using the Base64 decoder and move to a Caesar cipher solver. This made the process more systematic instead of repeatedly trying the same tool.


6. Security Takeaway
This challenge showed me why analysts need to recognize different forms of encoding and basic cryptography. Data can be wrapped in several layers, and each layer can require a different tool or method.
It also reinforced the importance of separating evidence from terminal prompts and other unrelated text. When documenting an investigation, the actual decoded content should be clearly labeled so another person can tell what came from the evidence and what came from the command-line environment.


7. What I Learned
I learned that decoding can involve multiple layers, so I should not assume the first successful conversion is the final answer. I also got better at recognizing the difference between Base64-encoded data and Caesar-cipher text.
For future investigations, I would inspect and label each output before moving to the next step. I would also make sure my GitHub evidence is sanitized and stored in the correct portfolio folder before publishing it.
